<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.Description is capped at 255 characters and the rationale below is far
        longer; a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. NOTE ALSO: the
        comment must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment placed ABOVE <ValidationRule>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml
        file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        ADDED 2026-08-20. WHY A VALIDATION RULE EXISTS AT ALL FOR SOMETHING THE UI ALREADY
        SCOPES.
        =====================================================================================
        DispositionBrokerStampService's class header calls the off-market scoping "STRUCTURAL"
        and the on-market path "unreachable". That is true THROUGH THE UI ONLY. Broker__c is
        hidden on an on-market row by a fieldInstance visibilityRule on Disposition_Record_Page,
        and a visibilityRule is a rendering decision - it constrains a browser, not the API. A
        data load, a Flow, a REST call, a seed script or anonymous Apex can all set Broker__c on
        an On_Market row without ever consulting that page.

        WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THEY DO, IN ORDER:
          1. DispositionBrokerStampService fires (it keys on Broker__c CHANGING and carries NO
             record-type test of its own) and overwrites Selected_Broker__c with its off-market
             'Person - Firm' string.
          2. That string is the FIRM value the on-market BOV chain
             (BovSubmissionTriggerHandler / BovSubmissionService) had written there from
             BOV_Submission__c.Broker_Firm__c. It is now gone.
          3. DispositionStageEntryService then copies Selected_Broker__c straight into
             Broker_Listing__c.Broker_Firm__c on listing creation. A person's name is now
             sitting in a column whose name, meaning and every consumer say "firm".

        THIS IS ENFORCEMENT, NOT A DUPLICATE OF A DISPLAY RULE. The flexipage rule decides what
        renders; this rule decides what may be SAVED. They happen to agree on the criterion, and
        that agreement is the point - the API surface is now closed to the same population the
        UI already refuses, rather than being closed only by convention.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 WHY Is_On_Market__c AND NOT AN EXPLICIT RecordType.DeveloperName TEST. THE MASTER
        RECORD TYPE IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT.
        =====================================================================================
        The two candidate formulas differ ONLY on a Master-record-type row:

            AND(NOT(ISBLANK(Broker__c)), Is_On_Market__c)                  <- CHOSEN
            AND(NOT(ISBLANK(Broker__c)), RecordType.DeveloperName <> 'Off_Market')   <- REJECTED

        Is_On_Market__c is a formula checkbox reading RecordType.DeveloperName = "On_Market", and
        a Master-record-type row reads BLANK on that merge field, so the checkbox is FALSE there.
        Its own field header states this. The chosen form therefore blocks On_Market ONLY and
        PERMITS Master; the rejected form would have blocked Master too.

        BLOCKING MASTER WOULD HAVE BEEN A REGRESSION, NOT EXTRA SAFETY, FOR THREE REASONS:
          1. TestDataFactory.dispositionRecordTypeId DELIBERATELY DEGRADES TO NO RECORD TYPE
             when the record type is absent from the org or unavailable to the running user
             (`info.isAvailable()`), leaving RecordTypeId unset. Its header states the reason in
             terms: "an undeployed record type must not turn every disposition test in the repo
             red". A Master row carrying Broker__c is therefore a NORMAL fixture shape in a
             fresh scratch org, and the rejected form would have turned exactly the off-market
             broker tests red in exactly the orgs where the feature has not deployed - undoing a
             guarantee the factory was written to provide.
          2. Broker__c RENDERS on a Master row. The flexipage visibilityRule shows the field when
             Is_On_Market__c is FALSE, which includes Master. Refusing a save on a field the page
             is actively offering is a trap; refusing one on a field the page hides is a closed
             back door. The chosen form draws the line in the same place the UI draws it.
          3. THE HARM CHAIN NEEDS AN ON-MARKET ROW. Step 2 above - the overwritten BOV firm
             string - only exists where the BOV chain ran, and that chain is on-market. A Master
             row has no BOV firm value to destroy. The population this rule protects IS the
             On_Market population.

        ⚠ THE RESIDUE IS STATED, NOT HIDDEN: a Master-record-type row may hold Broker__c and
        this rule will not object. That is the same FAIL-OPEN posture already documented and
        accepted in Is_On_Market__c's own header and in Broker_Selection_Approval's entry
        criterion 2, and it is bounded the same way - DispositionService stamps a record type
        explicitly on every row it creates, so a Master disposition is a test or import artefact,
        not a production state.

        =====================================================================================
        WHY THERE IS NO ISCHANGED / ISNEW GUARD, DIVERGING FROM BOTH SIBLING RULES.
        =====================================================================================
        Wire_Complete_Before_Sale_Closes uses OR(ISNEW(), ISCHANGED(...)) and
        All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression uses ISCHANGED alone, because both gate a TRANSITION.
        This rule asserts an INVARIANT - "an on-market disposition never holds a broker Contact"
        - and a transition guard would leave two doors open: a record-type flip from Off_Market
        to On_Market while Broker__c stays set (Broker__c never changes, so ISCHANGED(Broker__c)
        is false), and any pre-existing bad row, which would then be permanently invisible to
        the rule.

        🔴 AN UNGUARDED RULE CANNOT TRAP A RECORD HERE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE. If a row
        ever does reach the forbidden state, EVERY subsequent save of it fails - including the
        machine writes (DispositionApprovalAdvanceQueueable, ContractExecutionService,
        RecordStageAdvanceService) which use Database.update(..., SYSTEM_MODE) and SYSTEM_MODE
        does NOT bypass validation rules. But the remedy - saving with Broker__c cleared - is
        itself a PERMITTED save, and the error message says so. The rule is self-clearing.
        ⚠ ONE EXCEPTION TO THAT, WORTH KNOWING: a row locked by a pending approval
        (recordEditability AdminOnly) cannot be edited by a non-admin, so the recall has to come
        first. See the measured impact below for why no such row exists today.

        =====================================================================================
        MEASURED IMPACT AT CREATION: ZERO ROWS. NOT ASSUMED.
        =====================================================================================
        Queried on usman-dpeg, 2026-08-20:
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM Disposition__c WHERE Broker__c != null                   -> 0
          SELECT COUNT(Id) ... WHERE Broker__c != null AND RecordType.DeveloperName =
              'On_Market'                                                                -> 0
          GROUP BY RecordType.DeveloperName over all Disposition__c                       ->
              On_Market 8, and nothing else. Zero Off_Market rows, zero Master rows.
        So this rule refuses nothing that exists. 🔴 THAT MEASUREMENT AGES. Re-run the first
        query at deploy time rather than citing this line - a non-zero result means an existing
        row would be frozen by the deploy until someone clears its broker, and that is an
        escalation, not a deploy.

        =====================================================================================
        NO errorDisplayField, DELIBERATELY.
        =====================================================================================
        Both sibling rules on this object anchor to Disposition_Stage__c because that is the
        field the user was editing. The offending field HERE is Broker__c, which is HIDDEN on
        every row this rule can fire on, and a violation typically arrives from an API caller
        with no page at all. A page-level error is therefore the honest placement.
        Record_Type_Is_Immutable on Account and Exactly_One_Parent on Deal_Message__c are the
        in-repo precedent for omitting the tag.
    -->
    <fullName>Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks Broker__c on an On_Market row. The off-market stamp would overwrite the BOV firm string in Selected_Broker__c, and DispositionStageEntryService copies that value into Broker_Listing__c.Broker_Firm__c, seeding a person name into a firm column.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    NOT(ISBLANK(Broker__c)),
    Is_On_Market__c
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>Broker is the off-market path only. This disposition uses the On Market record type, where the broker comes from the approved BOV submission and Selected Broker is stamped with that firm. Clear Broker, then appoint the broker on the BOV submission.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
